  "explanation": {
    "id": "b584ea48-55cf-4d7d-93d9-a628639f6ed1",
    "body": "Conditional convergence evaluates if the given alternating series converges ONLY as an alternating series. \nSeries $(i)$ is a geometric series whose $r$ value is less than 1, so the series converges absolutely. Absolute convergence and conditional convergence are mutually exclusive, so Series $(i)$ is excluded from our possibilities. \nSeries $(ii)$ is a p-series whose p value is less than 1. Thus, under normal circumstances, $(ii)$ would diverge. However, because it is alternating and its terms go to 0, the series converges conditionally. \nSeries $(iii)$ would diverge under normal conditions. However, the terms still go to zero and the series alternates, so the series converges conditionally. ",
    "questionId": "f13cb17f-9f28-447c-93c5-c22564a8a791",
